78,0,0,0,.736,2.249,2.461,2.461,0,0,0,2.132,1.027,3.747,3.747,0,0,0,.776-.078,3.647,3.647,0,0,0,.629-.184,3.691,3.691,0,0,0,.446-.213c.123-.071.229-.138.32-.2l.136-.1c.077,0,.116.085.116.252a.7.7,0,0,1-.116.35,3.565,3.565,0,0,1-1.144.978,3.239,3.239,0,0,1-1.647.436,3.682,3.682,0,0,1-2.762-1.211,4.125,4.125,0,0,1-1.153-2.955,4.552,4.552,0,0,1,1.1-3.218A3.586,3.586,0,0,1,276.163,255.012Zm-.195.717a1.708,1.708,0,0,0-1.541.863,2.919,2.919,0,0,0-.532,1.424c0,.091.038.136.116.136h3.78c.064,0,.1-.065.1-.194a2.717,2.717,0,0,0-.523-1.424A1.6,1.6,0,0,0,275.968,255.729Z\"></path><path d=\"M281.424,263.394a1.1,1.1,0,0,1-.339-.8,1.041,1.041,0,0,1,.339-.785,1.134,1.134,0,0,1,.805-.32,1.093,1.093,0,0,1,1.1,1.1,1.129,1.129,0,0,1-.32.8,1.035,1.035,0,0,1-.784.339A1.1,1.1,0,0,1,281.424,263.394Z\"></path></g></svg><div role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for triangle upper R upper T upper U and triangle upper S upper V upper U\" class=\"sr-only\"><ul>\n<li>Triangle upper R upper T upper U is labeled as follows:<br>\n<ul>\n<li>The measure of angle upper R upper T upper U is x°.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The 2 triangles partially overlap:<br>\n<ul>\n<li>Side upper R upper T of triangle upper R upper T upper U intersects side upper S upper V of triangle upper S upper V upper U.</li>\n<li>Vertex upper V of triangle upper S upper V upper U lies on side upper R upper U of triangle upper R upper T upper U.</li>\n<li>Vertex upper T of triangle upper R upper T upper U lies on side upper S upper U of triangle upper S upper V upper U.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>A note indicates the figure is not drawn to scale.</li>\n</ul></div></figure></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In the figure, <math alttext=\"upper R upper T equals upper T upper U\"><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>U</mi></math>, the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper V upper S upper T\"><mi>V</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>T</mi></math> is&nbsp;<math alttext=\"29 degree\"><mn>29</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, and the measure of angle&nbsp;<math alttext=\"upper R upper V upper S\"><mi>R</mi><mi>V</mi><mi>S</mi></math> is&nbsp;<math alttext=\"41 degree\"><mn>41</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. What is the value of <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math>?</p>","type":"spr","correct_answer":["156"]},"createDate":1730147357407,"updateDate":1730147357407}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147357407, 1730147357407),
    ($SATQ$2d521ca9$SATQ$, $SATQ$94dce764-2378-4b26-b4b0-980f97c7224f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Circles$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The measure of angle <math alttext="upper Z"><mi>Z</mi>
</math> is <math alttext="60 degree"><mn>60</mn>
<mo>&#176;</mo></math>. What is the measure, in <span style="text-decoration: underline;">radians</span>, of angle <math alttext="upper Z"><mi>Z</mi>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4c476f55-9a00-47e0-985d-4447a0897089","content":"<p><math alttext=\"one sixth pi\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>1</mn>\n\t<mn>6</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"823450aa-e3a7-4ba0-bb14-b3e145be225d","content":"<p><math alttext=\"one third pi\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>1</mn>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"59e09358-fe35-43c5-85be-e17177f05d34","content":"<p><math alttext=\"two thirds pi\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"8404fd1a-795e-4596-91e4-16c6474dc3b1","content":"<p><math alttext=\"1 pi\"><mn>1</mn>\n<mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["823450aa-e3a7-4ba0-bb14-b3e145be225d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is correct. The measure of an angle, in radians, can be found by multiplying its measure, in degrees, by <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="StartFraction pi Over 180 EndFraction"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>180</mn></mfrac></math>. It's given that the measure of angle&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper Z"><mi>Z</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="60 degree"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It follows that&nbsp;the measure, in radians, of angle&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper Z"><mi>Z</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="60 left parenthesis StartFraction pi Over 180 EndFraction right parenthesis"><mn>60</mn><mfenced><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>180</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="one third pi"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect. This is the measure, in radians, of an angle whose measure is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="30 degree"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, not <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="60 degree"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect. This is the measure, in radians, of an angle whose measure is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="120 degree"><mn>120</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, not <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="60 degree"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect. This is the measure, in radians, of an angle whose measure is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="180 degree"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, not <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="60 degree"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2d521ca9","external_id":"94dce764-2378-4b26-b4b0-980f97c7224f","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"96c6ae0a-1b18-4474-829e-03cb75988f49","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.D.","skill_desc":"Circles","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"60 degree\"><mn>60</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math>. What is the measure, in <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">radians</span>, of angle <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math>?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4c476f55-9a00-47e0-985d-4447a0897089","content":"<p><math alttext=\"one sixth pi\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>1</mn>\n\t<mn>6</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"823450aa-e3a7-4ba0-bb14-b3e145be225d","content":"<p><math alttext=\"one third pi\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>1</mn>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"59e09358-fe35-43c5-85be-e17177f05d34","content":"<p><math alttext=\"two thirds pi\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"8404fd1a-795e-4596-91e4-16c6474dc3b1","content":"<p><math alttext=\"1 pi\"><mn>1</mn>\n<mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"}],"keys":["823450aa-e3a7-4ba0-bb14-b3e145be225d"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. The measure of an angle, in radians, can be found by multiplying its measure, in degrees, by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction pi Over 180 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>180</mn></mfrac></math>. It's given that the measure of angle&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"60 degree\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>. It follows that&nbsp;the measure, in radians, of angle&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"60 left parenthesis StartFraction pi Over 180 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mn>60</mn><mfenced><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>180</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"one third pi\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. This is the measure, in radians, of an angle whose measure is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"30 degree\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"60 degree\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. This is the measure, in radians, of an angle whose measure is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"120 degree\"><mn>120</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"60 degree\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. This is the measure, in radians, of an angle whose measure is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"180 degree\"><mn>180</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"60 degree\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1755117017721,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"2d521ca9","skill_cd":"S.D.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Circles","createDate":1755117017721,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"","external_id":"94dce764-2378-4b26-b4b0-980f97c7224f","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"96c6ae0a-1b18-4474-829e-03cb75988f49","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"type":"mcq","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"60 degree\"><mn>60</mn>\n<mo>&#176;</mo></math>. What is the measure, in <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">radians</span>, of angle <math alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi>\n</math>?</p>","keys":["823450aa-e3a7-4ba0-bb14-b3e145be225d"],"answerOptions":[{"id":"4c476f55-9a00-47e0-985d-4447a0897089","content":"<p><math alttext=\"one sixth pi\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>1</mn>\n\t<mn>6</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"823450aa-e3a7-4ba0-bb14-b3e145be225d","content":"<p><math alttext=\"one third pi\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>1</mn>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"59e09358-fe35-43c5-85be-e17177f05d34","content":"<p><math alttext=\"two thirds pi\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>2</mn>\n\t<mn>3</mn>\n</mfrac>\n<mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"8404fd1a-795e-4596-91e4-16c6474dc3b1","content":"<p><math alttext=\"1 pi\"><mn>1</mn>\n<mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"}],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. The measure of an angle, in radians, can be found by multiplying its measure, in degrees, by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction pi Over 180 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>180</mn></mfrac></math>. It's given that the measure of angle&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper Z\"><mi>Z</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" altte