d may result from calculation errors.</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959626,"updateDate":1691007959626,"correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale":true}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959626, 1691007959626),
    ($SATQ$35d37640$SATQ$, $SATQ$1c2c5aa2-c601-4e42-9804-f7298eb7c510$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Circles$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Point <math alttext="upper F"><mi>F</mi>
</math> lies on a unit circle in the <em>xy</em>-plane and has coordinates <math alttext="left parenthesis 1 comma 0 right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. Point <math alttext="upper G"><mi>G</mi>
</math> is the center of the circle and has coordinates <math alttext="left parenthesis 0 comma 0 right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. Point <math alttext="upper H"><mi>H</mi>
</math> also lies on the circle and has coordinates <math alttext="left parenthesis negative 1 comma y right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>, where <math alttext="y"><mi>y</mi>
</math> is a constant. Which of the following could be the positive measure of angle <math alttext="upper F upper G upper H"><mrow>
	<mi>F</mi>
	<mi>G</mi>
	<mi>H</mi>
</mrow>
</math>, in radians?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3e346fe6-a71c-4315-b150-340be6925cca","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 27 pi Over 2 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>27</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"d5f046bb-d0c8-490c-97c8-7dfad85c1c34","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 29 pi Over 2 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>29</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"f681030b-aad6-4f2b-99af-e8286f0a2b62","content":"<p><math alttext=\"24 pi\"><mrow><mn>24</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"23b67d42-512c-47e5-8090-fe59b216abd4","content":"<p><math alttext=\"25 pi\"><mrow><mn>25</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["23b67d42-512c-47e5-8090-fe59b216abd4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is correct. It's given that the circle is a unit circle, which means the circle has a radius of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="1"><mn>1</mn></math>. It's also given that point <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper G"><mi>G</mi></math> is the center of the circle and has coordinates <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 0 comma 0 right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;and that point <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper H"><mi>H</mi></math> lies on the circle and has coordinates <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis negative 1 comma y right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></math>. Since the radius of the circle is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="1"><mn>1</mn></math>, the value of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="y"><mi>y</mi></math> must be <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="0"><mn>0</mn></math>, as all other points with an <em>x</em>-coordinate of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="negative 1"><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></math> are a distance greater than <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="1"><mn>1</mn></math> away from point <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper G"><mi>G</mi></math>. Since <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper F"><mi>F</mi></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper H"><mi>H</mi></math> are points on the unit circle centered at <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper G"><mi>G</mi></math>, let side <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper F upper G"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> be the starting side of the angle and side <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper G upper H"><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math> be the terminal side of the angle. Since side <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper F upper G"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> is on the positive <em>x</em>-axis and side <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper G upper H"><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math> is on the negative <em>x</em>-axis, side <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper F upper G"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> is half of a rotation around the unit circle, or&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="pi"><mi>π</mi></math> radians, away from side <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper G upper H"><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>. Therefore, the positive measure of angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper F upper G upper H"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>, in radians, is equal to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="pi"><mi>π</mi></math> plus an integer multiple of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="2 pi"><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></math>. In other words, the positive measure of angle <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper F upper G upper H"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>, in radians, is an odd integer multiple of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="pi"><mi>π</mi></math>. Of the given choices, only <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="25 pi"><mn>25</mn><mi>π</mi></math> is an odd integer multiple of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="pi"><mi>π</mi></math>.</p>
<p>Choice A is incorrect. This could be the positive measure of an angle where the starting side is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper F upper G"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> and the terminal side contains the point <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 0 comma negative 1 right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, not <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis negative 1 comma 0 right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>.</p>
<p>Choice B is incorrect. This could be the positive measure of an angle where the starting side is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper F upper G"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> and the terminal side contains the point <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 0 comma 1 right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, not <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis negative 1 comma 0 right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>.</p>
<p>Choice C is incorrect. This could be the positive measure of an angle where the starting side is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="upper F upper G"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> and the terminal side contains the point <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis 1 comma 0 right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, not <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="left parenthesis negative 1 comma 0 right parenthesis"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"35d37640","external_id":"1c2c5aa2-c601-4e42-9804-f7298eb7c510","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"35d20123-da12-4a4c-9a9e-5f031719e12a","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"S","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill_cd":"S.D.","skill_desc":"Circles","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Point <math alttext=\"upper F\"><mi>F</mi>\n</math> lies on a unit circle in the <em>xy</em>-plane and has coordinates <math alttext=\"left parenthesis 1 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. Point <math alttext=\"upper G\"><mi>G</mi>\n</math> is the center of the circle and has coordinates <math alttext=\"left parenthesis 0 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. Point <math alttext=\"upper H\"><mi>H</mi>\n</math> also lies on the circle and has coordinates <math alttext=\"left parenthesis negative 1 comma y right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>, where <math alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi>\n</math> is a constant. Which of the following could be the positive measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper F upper G upper H\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>F</mi>\n\t<mi>G</mi>\n\t<mi>H</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, in radians?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3e346fe6-a71c-4315-b150-340be6925cca","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 27 pi Over 2 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>27</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"d5f046bb-d0c8-490c-97c8-7dfad85c1c34","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 29 pi Over 2 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>29</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"f681030b-aad6-4f2b-99af-e8286f0a2b62","content":"<p><math alttext=\"24 pi\"><mrow><mn>24</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"23b67d42-512c-47e5-8090-fe59b216abd4","content":"<p><math alttext=\"25 pi\"><mrow><mn>25</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"}],"keys":["23b67d42-512c-47e5-8090-fe59b216abd4"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. It's given that the circle is a unit circle, which means the circle has a radius of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn></math>. It's also given that point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G\"><mi>G</mi></math> is the center of the circle and has coordinates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 0 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;and that point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper H\"><mi>H</mi></math> lies on the circle and has coordinates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis negative 1 comma y right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></math>. Since the radius of the circle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn></math>, the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi></math> must be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn></math>, as all other points with an <em>x</em>-coordinate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 1\"><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></math> are a distance greater than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn></math> away from point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G\"><mi>G</mi></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F\"><mi>F</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper H\"><mi>H</mi></math> are points on the unit circle centered at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G\"><mi>G</mi></math>, let side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> be the starting side of the angle and side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G upper H\"><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math> be the terminal side of the angle. Since side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> is on the positive <em>x</em>-axis and side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G upper H\"><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math> is on the negative <em>x</em>-axis, side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> is half of a rotation around the unit circle, or&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"pi\"><mi>π</mi></math> radians, away from side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G upper H\"><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>. Therefore, the positive measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G upper H\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>, in radians, is equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"pi\"><mi>π</mi></math> plus an integer multiple of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2 pi\"><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></math>. In other words, the positive measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G upper H\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>, in radians, is an odd integer multiple of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"pi\"><mi>π</mi></math>. Of the given choices, only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"25 pi\"><mn>25</mn><mi>π</mi></math> is an odd integer multiple of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"pi\"><mi>π</mi></math>.</p>\n<p>Choice A is incorrect. This could be the positive measure of an angle where the starting side is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> and the terminal side contains the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 0 comma negative 1 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis negative 1 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>.</p>\n<p>Choice B is incorrect. This could be the positive measure of an angle where the starting side is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> and the terminal side contains the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 0 comma 1 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis negative 1 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>.</p>\n<p>Choice C is incorrect. This could be the positive measure of an angle where the starting side is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> and the terminal side contains the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 1 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis negative 1 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1755117017716,"pPcc":"SAT#S","questionId":"35d37640","skill_cd":"S.D.","score_band_range_cd":7,"skill_desc":"Circles","createDate":1755117017716,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Geometry and Trigonometry","ibn":"","external_id":"1c2c5aa2-c601-4e42-9804-f7298eb7c510","primary_class_cd":"S","uId":"35d20123-da12-4a4c-9a9e-5f031719e12a","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"type":"mcq","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Point <math alttext=\"upper F\"><mi>F</mi>\n</math> lies on a unit circle in the <em>xy</em>-plane and has coordinates <math alttext=\"left parenthesis 1 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. Point <math alttext=\"upper G\"><mi>G</mi>\n</math> is the center of the circle and has coordinates <math alttext=\"left parenthesis 0 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>. Point <math alttext=\"upper H\"><mi>H</mi>\n</math> also lies on the circle and has coordinates <math alttext=\"left parenthesis negative 1 comma y right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>, where <math alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi>\n</math> is a constant. Which of the following could be the positive measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper F upper G upper H\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>F</mi>\n\t<mi>G</mi>\n\t<mi>H</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, in radians?</p>","keys":["23b67d42-512c-47e5-8090-fe59b216abd4"],"answerOptions":[{"id":"3e346fe6-a71c-4315-b150-340be6925cca","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 27 pi Over 2 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>27</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"d5f046bb-d0c8-490c-97c8-7dfad85c1c34","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 29 pi Over 2 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>29</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"f681030b-aad6-4f2b-99af-e8286f0a2b62","content":"<p><math alttext=\"24 pi\"><mrow><mn>24</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"23b67d42-512c-47e5-8090-fe59b216abd4","content":"<p><math alttext=\"25 pi\"><mrow><mn>25</mn></mrow><mi>&#960;</mi></math></p>"}],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. It's given that the circle is a unit circle, which means the circle has a radius of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn></math>. It's also given that point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G\"><mi>G</mi></math> is the center of the circle and has coordinates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 0 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;and that point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper H\"><mi>H</mi></math> lies on the circle and has coordinates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis negative 1 comma y right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></math>. Since the radius of the circle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn></math>, the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi></math> must be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn></math>, as all other points with an <em>x</em>-coordinate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"negative 1\"><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></math> are a distance greater than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn></math> away from point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G\"><mi>G</mi></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F\"><mi>F</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper H\"><mi>H</mi></math> are points on the unit circle centered at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G\"><mi>G</mi></math>, let side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> be the starting side of the angle and side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G upper H\"><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math> be the terminal side of the angle. Since side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> is on the positive <em>x</em>-axis and side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G upper H\"><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math> is on the negative <em>x</em>-axis, side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> is half of a rotation around the unit circle, or&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"pi\"><mi>π</mi></math> radians, away from side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G upper H\"><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>. Therefore, the positive measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G upper H\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>, in radians, is equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"pi\"><mi>π</mi></math> plus an integer multiple of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2 pi\"><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></math>. In other words, the positive measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G upper H\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>, in radians, is an odd integer multiple of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"pi\"><mi>π</mi></math>. Of the given choices, only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"25 pi\"><mn>25</mn><mi>π</mi></math> is an odd integer multiple of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"pi\"><mi>π</mi></math>.</p>\n<p>Choice A is incorrect. This could be the positive measure of an angle where the starting side is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> and the terminal side contains the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 0 comma negative 1 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis negative 1 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>.</p>\n<p>Choice B is incorrect. This could be the positive measure of an angle where the starting side is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> and the terminal side contains the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 0 comma 1 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis negative 1 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>.</p>\n<p>Choice C is incorrect. This could be the positive measure of an angle where the starting side is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> and the terminal side contains the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 1 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis negative 1 comma 0 right parenthesis\"><mo>(</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></math>.</p>","externalid":"1c2c5aa2-c601-4e42-9804-f7298eb7c510","correct_answer":["D"]},"createDate":1755117017716,"updateDate":1755117017716}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117017716, 1755117017716),
    ($SATQ$36200a38$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$14890-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Geometry and Trigonometry$SATQ$, $SATQ$S$SATQ$, $SATQ$S.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Lines, angles, and triangles$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p><img src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/36200a38/img_000_f088c374.png" alt="The figure presents a triangle with a horizontal base. The left and right sides of the triangle are both extended past the top vertex of the triangle. The bottom left angle is labeled 70 degrees, and the bottom right angle is labeled 50 degrees. The angle above the left side of the triangle and below the extension of the right side of the triangle is labeled x degrees." width="78" height="82">
